* START-HISTORY:
* 25 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 5315c65 (18 Oct 26) S.65 slice 3e: a file on another SD server (no pathname, FL$NETFILE 1) is asked of the kernel by its file variable, before the
*               administrator test; a local file is unchanged.
* 25 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux fc4030a (S.66): the refusal text comes from !edit_msg, which also knows the shared account's two refusals
*               (12518 a record of a shared file without shared.write, 12519 a structure change); the kernel's K$EDIT.OK / K$EDIT.KIND carry the
*               shared rule.
* 17 Oct 26 dm  New, S.64 (owner, 17 Oct 2026: the gates voc.edit, bp.edit and dict.edit).  The multi-user form of Linux Solo's
*               !voc_guard, which asks for ADMIN; this asks the session's gates and does not look at the file itself.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    deffun edit_guard(f) calling '!edit_guard'
*    if not(edit_guard(file.var)) then stop
*
* op_dio3.c refuses a user program's own WRITE, WRITEV, DELETE or CLEARFILE of a VOC, a dictionary or a program file when the session
* has not the gate for it.  The verbs that edit a file the USER names (ED, SED, CED, IDE, EDIT, COPY, COPYP, DELETE, CLEAR.FILE,
* CNAME, DELETE.FILE) are $internal, which the C test lets through because they have to be able to do their work, so each asks this
* before it changes a file it was given.  The verbs that keep the account's own records as a side effect of a command (CREATE.FILE's
* pointer, a saved sentence, the command stack, a compiled I-type) do not ask: the rule is DIRECT edits only.
*
* IT LOOKS AT THE FILE THAT WAS OPENED, NOT AT THE NAME THAT WAS TYPED, so a Q-pointer to a VOC, or "voc" in any case, is caught.  The
* kind comes from the kernel (K$EDIT.KIND on the pathname) and so does the answer (K$EDIT.OK), so the rules live in one place, op_sh.c.
* The administrator is never refused.
*
* Returns @true when the change may go ahead.  On @false it has already said why (12410 the VOC, 12411 a dictionary, 12412 a program
* file, 12518 a file of the shared account without shared.write, 12519 a structure change of one; 12415 when the file's pathname cannot be read, which is refused because a gate that fails open on a path it cannot read is
* decoration).
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function edit_guard(f)
$catalog !edit_guard

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   deffun edit_msg(emk) calling '!edit_msg'       ;* 18 Oct 26 dm, S.66

   p = fileinfo(f, FL$PATH)
   if p = '' then
*     18 Oct 26 dm - S.65 slice 3e: a file on another SD server has no pathname here.  The kernel knows its S.64 kind from the NAME the
*     remote file was opened by, so it is asked with the FILE VARIABLE (and it is the kernel that exempts the administrator).  A local
*     file answers 0 to FL$NETFILE and takes the old path below.
      if fileinfo(f, FL$NETFILE) then
         if kernel(K$EDIT.OK, f) then return @true
         kind = kernel(K$EDIT.KIND, f)
         if kind < 1 or kind > 3 then kind = 1
         display sysmsg(12409 + kind) ;* 12410 VOC, 12411 dictionary, 12412 program file
         return @false
      end
      if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then return @true
      display sysmsg(12415) ;* The pathname of the file cannot be read, so a change to it is refused
      return @false
   end

   if kernel(K$EDIT.OK, p) then return @true

   kind = kernel(K$EDIT.KIND, p)
   display edit_msg(kind) ;* 12410 VOC, 12411 dictionary, 12412 program file, 12518 shared record, 12519 shared structure
   return @false
end

* END-CODE
